58      static SingleFcn coercionFor(FType target, FValue v) {
59          if (target instanceof GenericTypeInstance) {
60              SingleFcn g = genericCoercionFor((GenericTypeInstance) target, v);
61              if (g != null) return g;
62          }
63          Fcn lifted = liftedCoercions(target);
64          if (lifted == null) return null;
65          List<FValue> args = Collections.singletonList(v);
66          if (lifted instanceof OverloadedFunction) {
67              return ((OverloadedFunction) lifted).bestMatchWithoutCoercion(args);
68          }
69          SingleFcn f;
70          if (lifted instanceof GenericFunctionOrMethod) {
71              GenericFunctionOrMethod g = (GenericFunctionOrMethod) lifted;
72              try {
73                  f = EvaluatorBase.inferByUnification(args, g, g.getWithin());
74              }
75              catch (FortressException ex) {
76                  return null;
77              }
78          } else if (lifted instanceof SingleFcn) {
79              f = (SingleFcn) lifted;
80          } else {
81              return null;
82          }
83          List<FValue> fargs = f.fixupArgCount(args);
84          return fargs != null && OverloadedFunction.argsMatchTypes(fargs, f.getDomain()) ? f : null;
85      }

== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## Landed semantics

FACTS.md:111
- **Under `walk`, the interpreter converts by coercion at its three kinds of type check** (2026-09-26, climb batch 4 rung C, `b628871a2`; `compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/REPORT.md`): a single function's, constructor's, method's or native's parameter (`NonPrimitive.typecheckParams` and the generic unification path); an overloaded call when no overload applies without coercion (`OverloadedFunction.bestMatchWithCoercion`, at the team's "TODO add checks for COERCE, right here." of `OverloadedFunction.java:792` at `47437c65f`), the most specific chosen by `Specification/basic/conversions-coercions.tex:477-484` and a call with no most specific refused as "Ambiguous coercion"; and a typed binding or assignment (`LHSEvaluator`, `BaseEnv`, `BuildEnvironments`, `LazilyEvaluatedCell`), where a variable, field or parameter declared with a tuple type converts element by element and an element already of its type is kept (`:708-719`). The conversions are the disambiguator's lifted `coerce_` functions, found at the declared type's top level and chosen on the run-time value; the converted arguments of an overloaded call are dispatched as an ordinary call, so the declaration that runs is the most specific for the converted value's run-time type (`Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:73-78`, `:466-468`; `ProjectFortress/tests/CoercionRedispatchRungC.fss`). An overloaded function keeps the coercions per argument types in its cache (`OverloadedFunction.java:760-776`) and re-dispatches every converted call; an overloaded method caches nothing, before the rung or after (`OverloadedMethod.mcache` is declared at `OverloadedMethod.java:28`, read at `:48` and `:56`, and written nowhere). Route A's price, in its general form: an argument whose static type needs a coercion while its run-time type plainly matches another overload dispatches plainly under `walk`, where the compiled run and the specification apply the coercion (`conversions-coercions.tex:525-536`, `:567-570`); gated as two expected failures, `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXCoercionStaticRungC.fss` (the specification's example, 4 under `walk` against 3) and `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXCoercionStaticNarrowRungC.fss` (`g(NarrowOf)` under `walk` against `g(Wide)` compiled, `compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/probes/skeptic/diff-1.txt:255`, `:263`). Not converted, each gated as an expected failure: a declared return type (row 387), a generic function's parameter (388), a generic trait's coercion (389), an overloaded function's tuple-typed parameter or an assignment to a tuple-typed field (395); varargs, keyword and arrow targets are not converted and not measured. Three interpreter tests print differently and nothing converts in any of them: `XXXimmutableTopLevel`, the Java line numbers of a printed stack trace through `LazilyEvaluatedCell.java` and `BaseEnv.java`, and `taskTrace2` and `taskTrace3`, identity hash codes (`Evaluator@hex`); all three are identical once those are normalised (`compile-ladder/rung-interp-coercion/probes/stay-green-repair.txt`), and they are the brief's stop, for Pavol.

== explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md, ## The harness and the gate

FACTS.md:86
- **An `XXX*.fss` in the interpreter corpus IS a gated expected-failure test** (established 2026-09-20 at the gather stage of climb batch 2, against rung W's record, which said the opposite and whose report is corrected). `ProjectFortress/tests/` holds 383 `.fss` and no `.test` file, and `FileTests.interpreterSuite("tests", true, false)` (`FileTests.java:768`) passes `expect_failure=false` — but that argument is the **fifth**, `knownFailure`; the `InterpreterTest` constructor at `:710-711` supplies the sixth itself, `super(path, d, s, unexpected_only, knownFailure, s.startsWith("XXX"))`, so `shouldFail` there comes from the file name, it is the same `final boolean shouldFail` field (`:69-70`, `:91`) that `:384` branches on, and `:400-402` fails the suite when such a file starts passing. 55 `XXX*.fss` already live in that directory, `XXXImportImportCollision.fss` and `XXXImportLocalCollision.fss` among them, and their api pairs come from `ProjectFortress/test_library/` (21 `.fsi`), so such a test needs neither a `.test` file nor a new api directory. What is true of `compiler_tests/` is the narrower constraint rung X recorded: there the mechanism runs through `TestTest`, and an `XXX*.test` cannot carry a `run_out_contains` key unless the failing run still prints the marker. The shape for a compiles-but-fails-at-run-time defect in that corpus is two `.test` files over one component, a plain-named one driving `link` and an `XXX`-named one driving `run`; rung B measured it and the gather used it for row 348's test, showing it go red on a deliberate local fix at `FileTests.java:587-589`.

